---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import CarLoanCalculator from '@/components/tools/finance/CarLoanCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="car-loan-calculator">
  <CarLoanCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to use the car loan calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Enter the <strong>negotiated vehicle price</strong>: the figure before tax and fees, not the
        sticker price and not the first monthly payment the dealer offered.
      </li>
      <li>
        Add your <strong>trade-in value</strong> and the amount you <strong>still owe</strong> on that
        vehicle. If you owe more than it is worth, the calculator flags the negative equity.
      </li>
      <li>
        Enter your <strong>cash down payment</strong>, the local <strong>sales tax rate</strong> and
        the <strong>fees</strong> for title, registration and documentation.
      </li>
      <li>
        Set the <strong>APR</strong> and the <strong>term in months</strong>. Try 48, 60 and 72 to
        see the real cost of a longer term.
      </li>
      <li>
        Compare the amount financed with the dealer's figure. If they differ, something was added to
        the deal.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Negotiate the price, not the payment</h2>
    <p>
      A longer term can make the monthly payment almost any amount, so do not negotiate the payment.
      Four numbers set the deal: the vehicle price, the trade-in allowance, the APR and the term. Fix
      the first two, compare offers for the third, and choose the shortest term you can afford.
    </p>
    <p>
      The calculator shows these separately. The out-the-door price is the cost of the car with tax
      and fees. The amount financed is what remains after your cash and trade-in. The total cost of
      borrowing is the interest on top. A dealer can lower one and raise another and keep the same
      payment.
    </p>

    <h2>What a longer term costs: 30,000 financed at 7.2% APR</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[420px] border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Term</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Monthly payment</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Total interest</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Extra vs 36 months</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="tabular-nums">
          <tr><td>36 months</td><td style="text-align:right">929.06</td><td style="text-align:right">3,446</td><td style="text-align:right">0</td></tr>
          <tr><td>48 months</td><td style="text-align:right">721.17</td><td style="text-align:right">4,616</td><td style="text-align:right">+1,170</td></tr>
          <tr><td>60 months</td><td style="text-align:right">596.87</td><td style="text-align:right">5,812</td><td style="text-align:right">+2,366</td></tr>
          <tr><td>72 months</td><td style="text-align:right">514.36</td><td style="text-align:right">7,034</td><td style="text-align:right">+3,588</td></tr>
          <tr><td>84 months</td><td style="text-align:right">455.72</td><td style="text-align:right">8,280</td><td style="text-align:right">+4,834</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      From 36 to 84 months, the payment falls by about half and the interest more than doubles. You
      also stay underwater much longer: a new car usually loses 20% or more of its value in the first
      year, and an 84-month balance falls very slowly.
    </p>

    <h2>Sales tax and the trade-in credit</h2>
    <p>
      In most US states, sales tax on a vehicle is charged on the price after the trade-in is deducted,
      so a 6,000 trade-in at a 6.5% rate saves 390 in tax plus the 6,000 itself. Some states, including
      California, Michigan (partially capped) and Virginia, do not give the full credit and tax the
      whole purchase price. The toggle in the calculator switches between the two methods.
    </p>
    <p>
      Outside the US, the equivalent is VAT or GST, which is usually included in the advertised price.
      In that case, set the tax rate to zero and enter the advertised figure as the vehicle price.
    </p>

    <h2>Negative equity</h2>
    <p>
      If you owe 14,000 on a car that the dealer values at 11,000, the 3,000 shortfall is added to the
      new loan. You then finance more than the new car is worth, pay interest on the debt of the old
      car for years, and start the next cycle further underwater. The calculator warns you when this
      happens and includes the rolled-in amount in the payment.
    </p>

    <h2>What is not included</h2>
    <p>
      This calculator covers financing, not running costs. Insurance, fuel or charging, tyres,
      servicing, depreciation and parking are not included, and together they usually cost more than
      the loan payment. If you finance gap insurance, extended warranties or paint protection, put them
      in the fees field. They are often the most profitable part of the deal for the dealer, and the
      easiest to decline.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
